00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:06.170
So to start, let me talk
a little bit about what

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:07.850
this session is really about.

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.100
It's really about
helping you understand

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.270
how to build scalable apps
using GCP and Firebase.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:19.070
And in particular, using the
Cloud Firestore database.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:22.250
Just to kick things
off, can I find out,

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:25.010
in this room, who has
used Firebase Real-time

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.320
Database before?

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.440
And who has used Cloud
Datastore before?

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:34.390
And who has already played
with Cloud Firestore?

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.220
I might need to get some of you
to come do the presentation.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.160
There's actually a lot of
people in here, that's awesome.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:48.320
So I want to start by painting
a picture or an architecture

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.449
of something that I would
have used to have done.

00:01:50.449 --> 00:01:52.490
Let's say you're in a
company and you're building

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.680
a new mobile web application.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.050
How would you go about building
that in a traditional way?

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.250
You would start off with a web
server for your static content.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.530
Then you might have a
dedicated application server

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.590
for the dynamic content
that's changing and mutating

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.360
as your users are using it.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.729
You probably have a database
server running somewhere,

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:12.650
running on some sort
of persistent storage.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:18.380
Then you're probably thinking
about adding in replications

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.300
so you can do maintenance
without having

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.380
to bring everything down.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:24.850
Maybe give yourself some high
availability capabilities.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:26.810
It's not too hard to add.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:29.240
And then as your app starts
to get more successful,

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.580
you're probably thinking
about disaster recovery

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:32.840
and how would you build that?

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.660
Now you're worrying about
a standby data center.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.630
You've got VPNs and
carrier interconnect byte

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:39.890
that you're building.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.590
At this point, you've got some
pretty heavy infrastructure

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.950
that you've built and you've
got full-time people maintaining

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.920
that infrastructure.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.030
Well, what we want
to do is give you

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.810
tooling that abstracts
that away and gives you

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.940
something where you can
focus on just the app

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.020
that you're writing.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.540
So with Cloud Firestore
and some of our products

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.610
that we have in the
Firebase platform,

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:03.800
we can simplify that
final architecture

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.390
that I showed you with
a very simple setup.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.880
You can use Cloud Firestore
directly from your devices.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.740
And you can use Firebase
Hosting for storing and serving

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:13.970
the static content.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:18.380
And you can use Firebase Auth to
give you that secure interface

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:19.280
into it.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.200
And then finally, you
can use Cloud Functions

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.290
to expand into the many other
tools that we have within GCP.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.360
So when we built
Cloud Firestore,

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.280
we really believed in giving
tooling so that small teams can

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.420
accomplish big things.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.410
And by providing
those small teams

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:43.640
with powerful yet simple
tools that get out of the way,

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.000
we can give them a
foundation for success.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.120
And lastly, we want to make
sure this tooling is available

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.090
everywhere.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:56.810
So let's start off with some
of the basics behind Firestore.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:58.820
So we have Firebase
SDKs that we've

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:02.870
integrated with that give you
native client libraries on iOS,

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:06.320
web, and Android.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.330
We integrate with
Firebase Authentication

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:10.700
to handle the authentication
into the server.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.950
We have a declarative
security rules language

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:16.310
to allow you to control access
to your data based on not just

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:17.899
the properties of
the signed in user,

00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:21.300
but also, the information
in its database itself.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:22.970
We give you Cloud
Functions to integrate

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:24.770
into the rest of the platform.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.470
And we also use Firebase
Hosting as a convenient way

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:31.580
to serve up content.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:34.490
Now, it is expected
in apps today

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:37.730
that they just work
across mobile and web.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:40.070
You may not have
intended this to happen,

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.775
but it's a basic expectation
most people have today,

00:04:42.775 --> 00:04:44.150
that they can pick
up their phone

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.730
and continue using what
they were using on the web.

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:50.850
And sometimes even use both
of them at the same time.

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:52.820
There's a further
expectation, which, perhaps,

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:54.903
some people in the audience
have discovered today,

00:04:54.903 --> 00:04:59.190
that network connectivity
is not always available.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:02.390
There are environments, even
in our hyper-connected world,

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:05.207
where you need to have offline
capabilities in your app.

00:05:05.207 --> 00:05:07.040
And we've built a lot
of tooling around this

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.350
to make it simple
to enable these use

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:13.460
cases in your application.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:17.690
Further to that, we know people
are building some very complex

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:20.960
applications on our tooling, so
we have a full suite of server

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:28.390
client libraries, including
C#, Go, Java, Python, PHP.

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:34.630
These particular toolings
allow you to use our serverless

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:39.040
database in a way that is truly
scalable, regardless of what

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.540
architecture you want to use.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.510
We have got a multi-region
replicated database

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:46.210
to make sure its always
reliable and available for you,

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.700
and I'll talk a little bit
about that in a second.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.845
So just to finish off the
actual database itself

00:05:53.845 --> 00:05:55.720
in this quick intro, I
want to talk about how

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:57.610
the data is structured.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:00.370
So we spent a lot of time with
the real-time database people

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.280
to understand how people
are using their data,

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.180
and what ways they want
to naturally use it.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.850
For Cloud Firestore, we're using
the collection and document

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:13.480
model that allows you to
store related data together

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:16.580
in a semi-structured fashion.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:20.610
So let's quickly talk
about what is new.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:23.350
And these are all coming
shortly, starting in August,

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:26.171
and then rolling
out through 2019.

00:06:26.171 --> 00:06:27.670
So the first one
that I want to talk

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:30.820
about, Cloud Firestore is going
to have a lot of new locations

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:31.570
launched.

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:33.640
So currently, we have
a multi-region location

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.860
in the US.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:37.420
And starting next
month, we're going

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:39.730
to gradually roll out the
full suite of locations

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:41.050
that you see here.

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:43.816
Whether it's in the Americas,
Europe, or Asia-Pacific,

00:06:43.816 --> 00:06:45.190
you're going to
be able to locate

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:47.380
your database and your
data closest to where

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:49.340
your particularly user base is.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:55.140
We are also announcing--
and this is of interest

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.450
to the Cloud Datastore
customers in the room--

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.280
that Cloud Firestore
is now going

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.370
to support Datastore mode.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:05.280
Cloud Firestore is designed
as the third generation

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:06.690
of Cloud Datastore.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:10.260
And it's going to support all
the existing APIs and client

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.480
libraries that you use today.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:15.379
We are going to seamlessly
upgrade every one,

00:07:15.379 --> 00:07:16.920
so you don't need
to change your code

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:18.900
and you don't need to
worry about downtime

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:22.140
to get the properties that
we have in this new system.

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:24.430
Some of those properties I
will go into in a second,

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:26.730
but they include things like
being strongly consistent

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:30.150
for all queries, and having
a five nines availability

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.150
SA once we get to GA.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:35.370
The beta for this new
mode starts in August.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:40.420
So you're probably
asking, what's the catch?

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:42.370
So let me talk about
a couple of the things

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:47.890
we're removing in Datastore
mode for Cloud Datastore users.

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:50.020
The first thing
that we're removing

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:53.260
is the entity group
restrictions you have today.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:55.510
So no longer will you
be limited to one write

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:59.200
per second per entity group to
get strongly consistent data.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:02.560
So hopefully, this is a good
thing we have removed for you.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:04.480
The second one is
you no longer need

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.780
to use ancestor queries to
have strong consistency.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:10.540
All queries across
Cloud Datastore,

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:12.880
when you're using Cloud
Firestore in the new Datastore

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.570
mode, will be
strongly consistent.

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.070
And the last thing
we're removing

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:22.100
is the 25 entity group
limit for transactions.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:24.070
It doesn't matter how
many entity groups

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:26.740
you use in Cloud Datastore, we
will support the transactions

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:29.250
for it.

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.000
Just want to quickly
touch on the upgrade,

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.666
because I know a lot
of you who have got

00:08:32.666 --> 00:08:34.350
Cloud Datastore
databases are wondering

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:36.400
how this is going to happen.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:39.120
So we are doing the
upgrade for you.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.679
The Google engineering
team is going

00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:44.070
to be live migrating the
information from Cloud

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:46.890
Datastore to Datastore
mode without any downtime.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:50.970
We will send announcements out
about this to our user base

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:52.410
in the coming weeks.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.270
There is no action
required on your behalf.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:57.150
We are going to
handle everything.

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.760
There are no code changes
that you need to do.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:02.370
And all the existing
functionality APIs and client

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:05.794
libraries you use
will still work.

00:09:05.794 --> 00:09:07.210
If you want to
play with it early,

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:10.630
you can create new databases
during our beta period.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:15.200
And once we get to GA,
we will start upgrading.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:16.950
So I just wanted to
quickly point out some

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:19.470
of the differences in case
you're wondering when you would

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:21.810
use Datastore mode-- it's
the new mode we're announcing

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:22.620
today--

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:24.660
and native mode, which
is the existing mode

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.710
Cloud Firestore users
have been using in beta.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:30.150
There's a couple of minor
terminology differences

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:31.410
between them.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:35.640
Datastore mode is designed
as that server-side database,

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:39.690
and the native mode is designed
as your backend-as-a-service

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:42.690
for your mobile, web,
and IoT applications.

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:45.660
When you think about Datastore,
think about a NoSQL database

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:48.990
that scales from zero to
a million writes a second.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:50.700
When you think
about Native mode,

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:52.530
think about a
backend-as-a-service that

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:55.304
scales from zero to a million
concurrent connections from

00:09:55.304 --> 00:09:55.845
your devices.

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:02.550
We have also just
published our draft SLA.

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:07.910
So this is the SLA that will go
into effect once we get to GA.

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:09.690
For our multi-region
locations, it's

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.310
five nines of availability.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.430
And for our regional
locations, it's

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:15.150
four nines of availability.

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:18.432
This system is designed to make
sure your app is up and running

00:10:18.432 --> 00:10:19.140
when you need it.

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:25.470
In August, we are also
releasing managed exports

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:26.670
for native mode.

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.520
So this will allow you to take
a full export of your data

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:32.490
into GCS and import it
back into your project

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:34.600
if you need to recover
some of that data.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:37.950
Or perhaps, import it into
another project, a dev or a QA

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:39.150
environment.

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:43.700
You can also take these exports
and import them into BigQuery

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:47.190
so you can use a full SQL
language to do ad hoc reporting

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:48.090
against that data.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:55.500
We have also added the
data viewer for native mode

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:57.480
into GCP's console.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.690
So starting in August, you
will see a new Firestore tab

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.660
where you can see our live
updating UI to manipulate

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:06.830
and query against your data.

00:11:06.830 --> 00:11:09.630
We are also adding a
new set of functionality

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:12.180
around managing your indexes.

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:15.000
So for those who haven't
used Cloud Firestore before,

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.080
we automatically index all
your fields in your document.

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:21.910
Sometimes, you want
more control over that,

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:23.550
and so we've got
a new system that

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:27.120
allows you to selectively turn
on and off particular types

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:31.532
of indexes for your document.

00:11:31.532 --> 00:11:33.240
For those people who
have got sharp eyes,

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:36.960
you may have seen a new indexing
mode down the bottom, called

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:38.260
array contains.

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:41.430
So Cloud Firestore will now be
able to do containment queries

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:42.550
against arrays.

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:45.060
And I just saw a little fist
bump, so that's a good sign.

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:52.370
So now I want to talk about
some of the common architectures

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:54.860
I've seen with customers
who have been using

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:57.390
Cloud Firestore successfully.

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:00.600
The first one I want to
talk about is Guesswork.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.890
Guesswork is a machine
learning startup

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:07.520
for helping e-commerce
companies in emerging markets.

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:10.820
They recommend new products
for first time buyers

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:13.550
on their site.

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:15.890
When a new user
arrives on the site,

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:20.300
Guesswork collects a whole bunch
of datapoints about that user,

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:21.600
where they're coming from.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.620
Did they come from an email
campaign or a fashion website?

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:27.200
And including other things
like the geographic region

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:28.175
and the device type.

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:32.750
They then come up
with a recommendation

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:33.870
based on this user.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:35.660
And the data can take
a couple of paths

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:37.230
to get back to the user.

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:39.860
So let's have a
quick look at one

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:43.190
of the examples they did
with a company called Zalora

00:12:43.190 --> 00:12:46.040
in Southeast Asia,
to bring better

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:50.850
e-commerce recommendations
to their customers.

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.570
So to start with, they were
using the Google Tag Manager--

00:12:53.570 --> 00:12:55.160
you may be familiar
with this, if you

00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:57.380
are using Firebase Analytics--

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:00.830
with the Cloud Firestore SDK
to write information back

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:04.940
into Cloud Firestore about
the user who visited the site.

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:07.790
This then triggers a couple
of different data pipelines

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:09.780
to process this data.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:14.960
The first one is triggering
a Cloud Function that then

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:17.120
inserts the data into BigQuery.

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:19.040
This makes the whole
dataset available

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:23.270
to their recommendation
engine to do processing.

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:26.040
The second pipeline
that gets kicked off

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:28.850
with a Cloud Function is
using the Cloud Vision

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:31.040
API to process the image.

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:33.110
Now, this, I think,
is really interesting.

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:36.740
Because Guesswork deals with
so many different languages

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:40.640
out there, they found it more
reliable to use the Vision

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:45.470
API across the image of the
product to get metadata back,

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:47.570
than relying upon
the text metadata

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:49.280
and trying to
understand and process

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:51.340
all the different
variations of language.

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:53.090
So you get better,
more consistent results

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:56.540
by processing the
image of the product.

00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:59.780
The recommendation then
uses this information

00:13:59.780 --> 00:14:00.710
to take two paths.

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:03.080
The first one is
for anonymous users.

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:05.930
They write the recommendation
back into Firestore,

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:08.140
which gives a
real-time update out

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:10.260
to the client for that user.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:14.180
For registered users, they
can send a periodic email

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:15.590
with the
recommendations to them.

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:23.240
So here is the whole
architecture on a single slide.

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:26.240
It's very simple,
but very powerful.

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:27.860
And this architecture
has allowed

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:30.200
them to move very
quickly and make changes.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:33.590
So they can AB test
different algorithms

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:37.250
to improve their recommendations
and improve, obviously,

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:39.240
their revenue.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:42.680
So one of the things that I
really love about this example

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:45.920
is Guesswork is a
company of three people

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:48.620
that they bootstrapped
using this technology.

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:52.100
And they now serve
100 million users.

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:55.700
So three people managed to
bootstrap a successful company

00:14:55.700 --> 00:15:00.160
to serve 100 million
users using this tech.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:04.180
That's all about the small
teams able to do big things.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:08.680
And this is another example
I love, Hawkin Dynamics.

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:11.740
They're probably one of the
most interesting meetings

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:12.860
I've been on.

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:14.860
You'll see some
force plates on here.

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:16.720
We had a video
conference with them

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:18.820
and they were in the
back of the room jumping

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:20.960
up and down, doing
counter-movement jumps

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:22.100
on plates.

00:15:22.100 --> 00:15:24.880
It's probably one of the most
energetic meetings I've had.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:27.550
So Hawkin Dynamics is a
sports performance company

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:29.230
focused on providing
cutting edge

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:35.110
technology in both software and
hardware for athletic teams.

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:39.970
They optimize
athletic performance

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:43.210
by analyzing the information
from these force plates using

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:46.220
things like counter-movement
jumps and isometric tests.

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:51.160
And so let's have a quick look
at their architecture, which

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:52.030
is very simple.

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:54.030
And there's one little
bit I'll add in a minute,

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:56.080
but it really is
this simple for them.

00:15:56.080 --> 00:16:00.820
So they've got force plates that
they give out to teams that,

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:03.050
at a very fine
grain level, measure

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:06.160
of the force of the
movements of the athletes.

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:09.100
This information is then
wirelessly transmitted

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:10.390
to the mobile apps--

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:15.310
whether it's a phone or a
tablet that Hawkin Dynamics has.

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:17.320
These apps work
offline because not

00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:20.950
all the locations where
they're doing this test

00:16:20.950 --> 00:16:23.170
has network connectivity.

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:25.990
On the app, they can
quickly review the raw data

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:27.520
that the force
plates has produced

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:30.100
and maybe get the athletes
to repeat the test

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:32.260
or look for something new.

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:34.690
When they are connected,
they use Firebase Auth

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:37.030
to authenticate in and
sync this information

00:16:37.030 --> 00:16:40.450
into Cloud Firestore, which,
once again, kicks of Cloud

00:16:40.450 --> 00:16:43.240
Functions to do the
processing on the raw data,

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:46.540
to pull out moving averages
and other information

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:49.900
that then they can display in
more depth in their mobile web

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:50.960
application.

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:53.200
So when they are
online, all of these

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:55.080
are kept closely in
sync with each other,

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:58.517
so you can see in real-time
what the results are.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:03.437
They sent me a copy
of their data model

00:17:03.437 --> 00:17:05.770
so we can have a quick look
and see how they structured.

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:07.930
And this uses some of the
collection and document

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:10.923
hierarchy that we provide
within Cloud Firestore.

00:17:10.923 --> 00:17:12.339
So they have an
overall collection

00:17:12.339 --> 00:17:14.380
called teams, where
all of the sports teams

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:15.379
they have live within.

00:17:15.379 --> 00:17:17.920
And they have a document that
just has some basic information

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:19.569
about that team.

00:17:19.569 --> 00:17:21.760
Under the team
document, each team

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:23.619
has a set of data,
the settings for how

00:17:23.619 --> 00:17:25.780
they want to configure
the displays and the tests

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:28.040
that they have, the
actual raw data that

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:31.480
gets synced from the force
plates, the test, which

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:35.420
is a computed result that
comes from Cloud Functions,

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:37.420
and the different groups,
the different athletes

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:41.420
within those players
on that team exist.

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:43.430
So let's talk
about the athletes,

00:17:43.430 --> 00:17:45.910
which has a document that
outlines a bunch of information

00:17:45.910 --> 00:17:48.100
about when they were
tested using arrays,

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:51.350
what different groups they're
in, what their weight is,

00:17:51.350 --> 00:17:52.360
et cetera.

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:54.870
And then under that is
that computed average doc,

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:58.420
so a player can go in and look
at their performance over time

00:17:58.420 --> 00:18:00.910
and how they're improving
based on different training

00:18:00.910 --> 00:18:01.510
schedules.

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:06.590
So with that, I want to
hand it back to Kathy,

00:18:06.590 --> 00:18:12.072
to talk about how Google has
been using Cloud Firestore.

00:18:12.072 --> 00:18:13.460
KATHY LI: Thanks, Dan.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:15.950
So if you're looking
at this screenshot,

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:20.300
this page is probably, one
that you actually know and have

00:18:20.300 --> 00:18:22.040
already interacted with.

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:25.110
It's the Google Cloud
Next conference Website.

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:27.440
Our developer friends
at the Nerdery

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:30.050
actually built the
web and mobile apps

00:18:30.050 --> 00:18:34.260
for this event using
Cloud Firestore.

00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:37.130
They, like many
developers often do, work

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:40.070
with existing systems
and storage solutions,

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:42.380
rather than building
from the ground up.

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:47.120
Even so, Cloud Firestore can
still work well in this case.

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:50.780
We'll first take a look at
some of their actual data

00:18:50.780 --> 00:18:54.350
in Firestore to give an
example of how some application

00:18:54.350 --> 00:18:55.950
data can be laid out.

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:57.770
And then we'll zoom
to the whole system.

00:19:00.750 --> 00:19:05.800
This is a view of the Firestore
Data Viewer in the Firebase Web

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:06.570
Console.

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:09.990
And you can see there
are top level collections

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:14.160
for, in this case, events, with
documents for the Cloud Next

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:17.040
events in San Francisco,
Tokyo, and London.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.680
And the latter two are
coming up in the fall.

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:21.900
It's a flexible data
model so your application

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:23.400
can write whatever you need.

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:26.220
In this case, there are
some metadata fields

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:31.140
on the individual
days of this event.

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:34.200
We also have some English
language subcollections

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:37.500
of all the content that you'll
see on the conference site,

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:39.990
such as the home page and
marketing copy, the event

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:42.510
sponsors, and the
filters that you

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:47.550
use to narrow down your view
of the day's programming.

00:19:47.550 --> 00:19:51.810
Lastly, here are the
sessions in a subcollection,

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:54.720
with documents for every one of
the general sessions that you

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:57.960
might attend, including the
one we're sitting in right now.

00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:02.460
There's the document
with a list of labels

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:05.670
for the topics, a room capacity.

00:20:05.670 --> 00:20:09.420
And as you might
imagine, if the size

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:11.640
of the attendee
list for a session

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:15.090
reaches the room capacity,
then the application logic

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:17.695
will set the can reserve
Boolean field to false.

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:23.140
So now that you've seen a
view of some of the data,

00:20:23.140 --> 00:20:26.380
how do the developers use
Cloud Firestore to improve

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:29.950
on the app from prior years?

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:34.750
Nerdery already works with
an existing third-party SAS

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:38.110
provider that specializes
in event management.

00:20:38.110 --> 00:20:41.360
So they store all
the conference data,

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:46.450
such as sessions, speakers,
registrants, rooms, and map

00:20:46.450 --> 00:20:48.460
data.

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:50.690
They can read
directly from here,

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:52.900
but since there's no
event-driven model

00:20:52.900 --> 00:20:56.080
for responding to
changes in your data,

00:20:56.080 --> 00:21:00.820
the app pulls this information
on the order of many minutes,

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:05.680
which in the past has led to
registrants quickly filling up

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:08.350
sessions on the day
of, and a slight delay

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:09.715
in showing that status.

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:15.240
Now, Nerdery copies
all of their event data

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:19.560
into Cloud Firestore, which they
use as a single source of truth

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:22.480
for all apps to read from.

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:25.540
They also wrote a custom
API served on Google App

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:28.710
Engine, which accepts
writes from clients,

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:31.800
such as a user
bookmarking a talk,

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:36.570
and handles transactions
to the third-party system.

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:39.810
Then the clients read
directly from Firestore

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:42.090
to display all the data
that you actually see.

00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:46.950
Here's a view of
the whole system .

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:49.140
And we can contextualize
it with an interaction

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:51.780
that you've probably
already had.

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:54.750
When you first view
the conference website,

00:21:54.750 --> 00:22:00.210
it loads all the session
and information on partners

00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:02.850
and stuff from
Firestore, as well as

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:07.740
images and other static assets
from Google Cloud Storage.

00:22:07.740 --> 00:22:09.540
Then when you want
to take an action,

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:11.640
like bookmarking a
panel discussion,

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:15.840
you'll log in using either
your Google account or email

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:16.620
address.

00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:20.610
And the apps use Firebase
Auth to authenticate you.

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:23.280
Then when you register for
a session, such as this one,

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:29.040
the app makes an API call
out to the third-party system

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:33.550
to update the logged in
user's set of reserved events.

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:37.410
But it also immediately writes
that session metadata back

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:39.150
to Firestore.

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:42.390
This eliminates having to
poll on session availability,

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:45.840
so that as soon as an
event actually fills up,

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:49.500
its status will be displayed
as full to all the apps.

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:52.380
So thanks to the folks over
at Nerdery for building

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:54.180
the apps that we're
all using this week.

00:22:57.630 --> 00:23:01.880
Now we want to show
you the scalability

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:04.970
and ease of use of
Cloud Firestore.

00:23:04.970 --> 00:23:07.820
We have a demo that
illustrates how

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:11.570
a large number of
concurrently connected devices

00:23:11.570 --> 00:23:15.860
can receive updates from and
write updates to Firestore.

00:23:19.150 --> 00:23:23.650
This will be visualized shortly
in a Google-colored Firestore

00:23:23.650 --> 00:23:28.180
logo display with pixels
that represent the end user

00:23:28.180 --> 00:23:32.410
devices in each of
four colored quadrants.

00:23:32.410 --> 00:23:36.640
Then to reflect the quadrants'
updated colors in real-time,

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:39.260
we'll use one of the major
features OF Firestore, which

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:41.830
is the ability to
listen for changes

00:23:41.830 --> 00:23:44.380
at any node in your
data hierarchy.

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:46.690
So you'll see pixels
updating their color

00:23:46.690 --> 00:23:50.785
as new color values are
written to the documents

00:23:50.785 --> 00:23:51.535
for each quadrant.

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:57.030
If you want to see
this visualization,

00:23:57.030 --> 00:23:59.400
please visit firepixels.app.

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:04.680
And also, hold up your device
to us when it's loaded.

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:06.850
I will do this as well.

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:09.210
So you should see something
kind of like this.

00:24:17.080 --> 00:24:20.565
Awesome, seeing a
few following along.

00:24:24.672 --> 00:24:25.380
Thanks, everyone.

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:27.600
Can we also transition
to the demo screen?

00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:35.840
Here what we're
seeing is that we

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:41.180
imitate devices, which are
our clients, with Node.js

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:43.790
processes.

00:24:43.790 --> 00:24:46.400
And every simulated
device gets assigned

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:51.710
a pixel in this entire grid,
totaling 40,000 pixels.

00:24:51.710 --> 00:24:55.700
Essentially, 40,000
concurrently connected devices.

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:59.000
Making a quadratic of
pixels change color

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:02.900
is done by writing a new
value to the color field

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:06.180
in a document for each quadrant.

00:25:06.180 --> 00:25:10.010
Then we set up the clients
to listen on the input

00:25:10.010 --> 00:25:11.510
document for each quadrant.

00:25:11.510 --> 00:25:13.610
And when it gets
written, they'll

00:25:13.610 --> 00:25:16.080
read the new color
from the data snapshot

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:20.000
and write it back to the
pixel document in Firestore.

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:22.310
As the pixels' color
values are written,

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:24.650
you'll then see their
corresponding pixels

00:25:24.650 --> 00:25:27.350
in the display change color.

00:25:27.350 --> 00:25:31.160
Currently, you're seeing
some of these color changes

00:25:31.160 --> 00:25:34.370
every handful of
seconds as there

00:25:34.370 --> 00:25:37.430
are some automatic changes
happening, as well as people

00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:41.270
probably playing with the
demo in the storage showcase

00:25:41.270 --> 00:25:43.940
booth, which is on the ground
floor of this building.

00:25:49.970 --> 00:25:53.530
So we can also
actually edit this--

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:57.760
or make the pixels and
quadrants change color ourselves

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:59.180
by writing the data.

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:01.900
And in order to do that,
let's actually bring up

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:02.810
the Cloud Console.

00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:12.260
So here's a view of the
Cloud Console we're in.

00:26:12.260 --> 00:26:16.471
We've navigated to the
Firestore Data Viewer.

00:26:16.471 --> 00:26:18.470
And over here, you can
see the three collections

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:23.900
that we have for
the many devices.

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:25.010
These are numbered.

00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:27.200
We have as many
simulated devices

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:31.220
as there are total spots with
an x and y-coordinate per

00:26:31.220 --> 00:26:32.970
quadrant.

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:38.240
We have pixels that get
assigned to each device.

00:26:38.240 --> 00:26:42.680
And each pixel is named
for its x and y-coordinate,

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:47.600
has a color which matches
the color of the input

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:51.710
document for the quadrant,
as well as a partition that's

00:26:51.710 --> 00:26:55.430
used to parallelize and
speed up the UI updates,

00:26:55.430 --> 00:26:57.890
and an updated time stamp.

00:26:57.890 --> 00:27:01.270
And we can look at
the input collection.

00:27:01.270 --> 00:27:06.230
So here are the documents zero
through three for all four

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:10.910
quadrants in the logo, with a
color that we can actually set.

00:27:10.910 --> 00:27:18.050
So right now, this
quadrant zero is green.

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:24.765
Although, this-- something
about doing demos.

00:27:28.977 --> 00:27:31.560
I'm going to check my phone real
quick to see if it's updated.

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:39.470
Let's see what happens when
we write this value directly

00:27:39.470 --> 00:27:43.040
in the Data Viewer, where you
can view real-time updates

00:27:43.040 --> 00:27:44.430
and modify your data.

00:27:58.070 --> 00:27:59.450
Sorry for the hiccup.

00:27:59.450 --> 00:28:04.100
So in this case, we actually--

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:05.790
If you're looking
at it on your phone,

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:07.640
you should see that
some of the quadrants

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:11.480
have pixels that
are mixed colors,

00:28:11.480 --> 00:28:13.010
or individual pixels
that aren't all

00:28:13.010 --> 00:28:14.510
the same color in the quadrant.

00:28:14.510 --> 00:28:17.630
And usually, these
represent the devices

00:28:17.630 --> 00:28:20.330
seeing that a new
value has been written

00:28:20.330 --> 00:28:22.160
for the color for that quadrant.

00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:24.710
And then, the clients
will asynchronously

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:28.700
pick up that update,
write their color back.

00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:31.160
And this then gets
updated in the UI, which

00:28:31.160 --> 00:28:35.840
is how you see different pixels
trickle into the new color

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:37.760
that they should be.

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:40.130
This will be going on
and you can actually

00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:44.780
play with this demo on
a few mobile devices

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:46.250
at the storage showcase booth.

00:28:49.130 --> 00:28:52.760
One thing we should be able
to see in the Data Viewer

00:28:52.760 --> 00:28:59.300
is that if you change the
value of the input here,

00:28:59.300 --> 00:29:04.580
the pixels should reflect
their respective quadrant,

00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:07.530
because it has a listener
on the input field.

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:14.890
So here, say we set
the input to blue.

00:29:14.890 --> 00:29:17.830
Then we go over to the
pixels, and it's now

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:19.930
been updated, whereas
it was red before,

00:29:19.930 --> 00:29:21.610
if you had noticed that.

00:29:21.610 --> 00:29:24.730
We changed this to green.

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:27.356
And then these values
should have-- oh,

00:29:27.356 --> 00:29:29.230
are updating in real-time
in the Data Viewer.

00:29:34.810 --> 00:29:36.880
And one other thing of
interest, especially

00:29:36.880 --> 00:29:39.670
for people who are
writing applications

00:29:39.670 --> 00:29:42.910
that use more complex
queries, and therefore,

00:29:42.910 --> 00:29:47.470
need to configure indexes
to support those queries,

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:52.210
you can navigate over
to the Indexes section.

00:29:52.210 --> 00:29:56.500
And here we have, in our case,
one composite index defined

00:29:56.500 --> 00:29:57.790
on the pixels collection.

00:29:57.790 --> 00:30:02.800
And two fields, which enables
a query use to load the UI.

00:30:02.800 --> 00:30:08.410
And this is the new feature
under Single Field indexes,

00:30:08.410 --> 00:30:12.430
where each project is
automatically configured

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:14.230
to have all of
their fields indexed

00:30:14.230 --> 00:30:17.800
in ascending, descending,
and array contains modes.

00:30:17.800 --> 00:30:22.360
But since you want to,
as a general pattern,

00:30:22.360 --> 00:30:27.040
avoid indexing, monotonically
increasing or decreasing

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:30.640
values, in this case, we
have disabled indexing

00:30:30.640 --> 00:30:34.360
on the fields that
represent time stamps--

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:37.150
last updated time stamps--

00:30:37.150 --> 00:30:40.510
to make sure that our app
keeps performing smoothly.

00:30:40.510 --> 00:30:42.180
Can we transition
back to the slides?

00:30:48.860 --> 00:30:55.040
As an overview of how
this app is set up,

00:30:55.040 --> 00:31:00.950
we have the devices simulated
as Node.js processes

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:03.950
running in Kubernetes
pods with separate pods

00:31:03.950 --> 00:31:05.630
for each quadrant.

00:31:05.630 --> 00:31:07.940
Each of those
processes then sets up

00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:11.720
many pixels, each
associated with a device,

00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:14.270
as well as setting up listeners
on the Firestore input

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:18.260
document that has the
color per quadrant.

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:21.230
We can change the
colors in the display,

00:31:21.230 --> 00:31:24.380
either using a
controller app that you

00:31:24.380 --> 00:31:29.720
can see-- it'll be on a pixel
phone app the demo downstairs--

00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:33.650
or via just changing the
data directly in the Cloud

00:31:33.650 --> 00:31:35.840
Console, which we just did.

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:39.500
This writes to Firestore,
which triggers more updates

00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:42.290
for each pixel document.

00:31:42.290 --> 00:31:46.370
Then with thousands of
pixels updating at once,

00:31:46.370 --> 00:31:50.090
we use a Golang backend
app to send the new colors

00:31:50.090 --> 00:31:53.930
to the Firestore logo display
using web sockets, which

00:31:53.930 --> 00:31:56.780
for us is a workaround so
that the browser doesn't

00:31:56.780 --> 00:32:00.350
try to process thousands
of updates at once.

00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:02.930
But you can see that
Firestore does scale

00:32:02.930 --> 00:32:06.121
to handle many reads
and writes in real-time.

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:11.680
To show this a little
more concretely,

00:32:11.680 --> 00:32:16.020
here's a code snippet using
a key feature of Firestore,

00:32:16.020 --> 00:32:18.780
which is the real-time updates.

00:32:18.780 --> 00:32:25.240
We have, at the top,
references to the database

00:32:25.240 --> 00:32:27.030
and each of the
collections in it.

00:32:27.030 --> 00:32:30.000
And in our init
function, we actually

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:33.030
call this setup
listener for each spot.

00:32:33.030 --> 00:32:38.910
This is per process
after we've spawned many.

00:32:38.910 --> 00:32:42.870
We set up a listener
on the input document

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:47.010
per each of the four areas, such
that when that gets written,

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:48.600
we invoke our callback.

00:32:48.600 --> 00:32:52.380
That grabs the data snapshot
and writes the new color value

00:32:52.380 --> 00:32:58.740
to the pixel document, which
then gets rendered in the UI.

00:32:58.740 --> 00:33:01.290
So there you have it.

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:06.030
We are using Firestore to
sync app state in real-time.

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:10.020
And it scales up to
tens of thousands

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:12.120
of concurrently
connected devices.

00:33:12.120 --> 00:33:14.820
And you can have,
ultimately, millions

00:33:14.820 --> 00:33:17.350
of clients using your app.

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:19.860
Now we'll hand it back
to Dan, to wrap up

00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:24.300
with some considerations to keep
in mind as you build and grow

00:33:24.300 --> 00:33:25.180
your Firestore apps.

00:33:29.447 --> 00:33:30.530
DAN MCGRATH: Thanks Kathy.

00:33:30.530 --> 00:33:32.780
So I want to just run through
a couple of quick points

00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:35.570
to keep in mind when you're
building larger apps, before we

00:33:35.570 --> 00:33:40.070
go into Q&amp;A. And I will not try
to do any demo on conference

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:42.640
Wi-Fi.

00:33:42.640 --> 00:33:46.430
So the first thing to
remember as we're learning,

00:33:46.430 --> 00:33:49.100
we've used a lot of
customer applications

00:33:49.100 --> 00:33:51.530
that are now part
of our test suite,

00:33:51.530 --> 00:33:53.570
and stress tests, in particular.

00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:56.480
So one thing to consider
when writing your application

00:33:56.480 --> 00:33:58.430
is how do you write scale?

00:33:58.430 --> 00:34:01.260
And so a couple of
guidelines that we have.

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:04.100
The first is for each
individual document,

00:34:04.100 --> 00:34:08.159
try to keep the sustained
update rate to once per second.

00:34:08.159 --> 00:34:10.130
So we do multi-region
replication

00:34:10.130 --> 00:34:13.920
across data centers that are
geographically dispersed.

00:34:13.920 --> 00:34:18.650
So there is some time of speed
of light considerations as part

00:34:18.650 --> 00:34:19.310
of that.

00:34:19.310 --> 00:34:21.150
You can, of course,
burst higher,

00:34:21.150 --> 00:34:23.360
but if you do a sustained
rate, like 30 a second

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:26.000
for a long period of
time, eventually your app

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:28.712
will start to get a lot slower.

00:34:28.712 --> 00:34:30.170
The other one which
Kathy mentioned

00:34:30.170 --> 00:34:33.469
with monotonically increasing
and decreasing values

00:34:33.469 --> 00:34:38.780
is keep the writes of those
types of data to around 500

00:34:38.780 --> 00:34:39.800
per second.

00:34:39.800 --> 00:34:43.489
When our system splits, we
do horizontal key ranges

00:34:43.489 --> 00:34:46.370
and we split on either side
to have more service take

00:34:46.370 --> 00:34:47.630
care of that load.

00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:50.330
When you're just appending
data to the end of, say,

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:53.780
indexes or primary keys
or prepending data,

00:34:53.780 --> 00:34:56.960
then all the load ends up
on one side of the split.

00:34:56.960 --> 00:34:59.360
So we'll only ever
have one server taking

00:34:59.360 --> 00:35:02.390
care of that load for you.

00:35:02.390 --> 00:35:07.310
When you're using the native
mode, we support, at the moment

00:35:07.310 --> 00:35:10.370
during beta, 10,000
writes per second.

00:35:10.370 --> 00:35:12.410
When you're in
Datastore mode, we

00:35:12.410 --> 00:35:15.690
support up to a million
writes per second.

00:35:15.690 --> 00:35:18.050
And this is around the
real-time notification system

00:35:18.050 --> 00:35:20.540
and whether that's
turned on or off.

00:35:20.540 --> 00:35:22.099
When you're ramping
up your traffic,

00:35:22.099 --> 00:35:24.140
so if you've got large
events that are happening,

00:35:24.140 --> 00:35:28.100
we have a rule that we call the
555 rule, which is starting off

00:35:28.100 --> 00:35:31.010
at 500 writes per second
and growing at 50%

00:35:31.010 --> 00:35:32.480
every five minutes.

00:35:32.480 --> 00:35:34.940
And for read, think
about 10x that.

00:35:38.670 --> 00:35:40.350
The other thing that
you should consider

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:42.540
is how your concurrent
connections work.

00:35:42.540 --> 00:35:45.097
So as your user base
scales, how are the number

00:35:45.097 --> 00:35:47.430
of concurrent connections
that you have from your client

00:35:47.430 --> 00:35:49.900
library going to scale?

00:35:49.900 --> 00:35:52.080
So as a general
rule, we support up

00:35:52.080 --> 00:35:54.420
to 1 million
concurrent connections

00:35:54.420 --> 00:35:58.605
when you're doing one update
per second per connection.

00:35:58.605 --> 00:36:02.400
So that's a million updates a
second across all your devices.

00:36:02.400 --> 00:36:05.790
To put that in context, a
million concurrent users

00:36:05.790 --> 00:36:10.930
is something like 10 to 14
million daily active users,

00:36:10.930 --> 00:36:14.142
depending on the makeup
of your application.

00:36:14.142 --> 00:36:16.350
The other thing to just keep
in mind is how much data

00:36:16.350 --> 00:36:18.510
you're pushing out
to your clients

00:36:18.510 --> 00:36:22.650
and generally, it's about
10 megabytes per second

00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:24.075
across that list of clients.

00:36:27.931 --> 00:36:31.646
[MUSIC PLAYING]

